<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Jerusalem</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 48–50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Apostolic Council</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, or Apostolic Council, is a meeting described in chapter 15 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a> and held in Jerusalem about 48–50 CE. According to Acts, it addressed whether Gentile converts had to be circumcised and keep the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a>, a question raised by the mission of Paul and Barnabas. It decided that they did not, but told them to abstain from food associated with idols, from blood and strangled meat, and from porneia; these instructions are known as the Apostolic Decree. Scholars increasingly affirm the council as historical, but disagree on whether Galatians 2 describes the same event.</p></div>
